8
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:56,160
Now, as you may remember, a few weeks ago, one of the Holo Talk listeners, Steve Michael, came on the show and he told us about his work producing laser holograms using a laser pointer, a $49 laser pointer that he picked up at Edmonds Scientific.

9
00:00:56,160 --> 00:01:01,920
He sent me two of the holograms, a laser transmission hologram and a reflection hologram, and they were beautiful.

10
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:09,840
So I was going to go down to Edmonds tomorrow, which is Monday, and take up one of these $49 laser pointers until I had a chance to see this ad.

11
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:15,840
Now this $7.99 laser pointer is called the Bullet Beam Laser Pointer.

12
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:36,800
It's manufactured by a company called Vector Manufacturing, and it is 5 milliwatts, it is 650 nanometers in wavelength, and it runs on three LM44 1.5 volt batteries, which totals out at about 4.5, or exactly 4.5 actually.

13
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:39,520
Now here's what here's the exciting part of this.

14
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:49,360
A lot of people think that you should get as close to the wavelength of the helium neon laser as possible to do holograms.

15
00:01:49,360 --> 00:02:06,120
But actually, with ACFA materials, if you were to ever look at their specification sheet and the response curve, the ACFA materials are actually more sensitive to the deeper red part of the spectrum than they are the part of the spectrum that the laser uh helium neon laser produces.

16
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:12,920
So it's not necessary to be close to 633 nanometers.

17
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:16,040
What you need is the coherence length of the diode itself.

18
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:23,400
Now, apparently this diode that Steve Michael bought has a lot of coherence to it because he's produced very deep holograms.

19
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:26,840
Now, whether this one does or not, I don't know, but I'm going to find out.

20
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:28,120
I actually bought two of them.

21
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:39,080
One I'm going to run off the batteries, and the other one I'm going to run off of a Radio Shack power supply here, which I notice I do have a 4.5 volt setting on it, so that I will never have to buy batteries again.

22
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:41,240
I'll just run it off of house current.

23
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:49,640
